/// Build the open effect as an `Eff(window)`; a boot system runs it (`gfx.open(w,h,title)(win:)`) and
/// inserts the handle into its Window pool.
open :: func(w: int, h: int, own title: []char) -> Eff(window) {
  return gfx_be_open(w, h, move title);
}

/// A window's dimensions, as ONE Eff via the applicative product `zip` of two backend reads. A draw system
/// binds both at once: `gfx.dims(handle)(w:, h:)`. The framebuffer is the driver-owned single-entity buffer
/// (`framebuffer :: [W*H]int`); gfx queries `{ framebuffer }` source-agnostically and indexes it directly.
dims :: func(h: window) -> Eff(int, int) {
  return zip(gfx_be_w(h), gfx_be_h(h));
}

/// The window's horizontal input axis as one Eff: -1 while a left key (←/A) is held, +1 while a right key
/// (→/D) is held, 0 otherwise. A step system binds it — `gfx.axis_x(handle)(dx:)` — and folds `dx` into a
/// position. Backends that have no keyboard wired (headless, wayland) report 0.
axis_x :: func(h: window) -> Eff(int) {
  return gfx_be_axis_x(h);
}

/// The window's VERTICAL input axis, the symmetric partner to `axis_x`: -1 while an up key (↑/W/Space) is
/// held, +1 while a down key (↓/S) is held, 0 otherwise. World +y is down, so "up" is negative — the same
/// convention as the screen.
///
/// This exists because HELD is not the same question as PRESSED, and `key` can only answer the latter. `key`
/// is a drained queue fed by OS auto-repeat (~30/s), so against a 60fps loop roughly every other frame finds
/// it empty. A driver polling `key` for a held jump therefore jumps only about HALF the times you land while
/// holding it — a coin flip, not a bug you can tune away. Level state has to come from level state.
axis_y :: func(h: window) -> Eff(int) {
  return gfx_be_axis_y(h);
}

/// The next discrete key press as one Eff — the typed ASCII byte (Enter=13, Backspace=8, Tab=9…) or a
/// GFX_KEY_* sentinel (arrows: left=1000, right=1001, up=1002, down=1003), or 0 when nothing is queued. A
/// step system drains it each frame — `gfx.key(handle)(k:)` — to feed an editor/text field. Non-blocking.
key :: func(h: window) -> Eff(int) {
  return gfx_be_key(h);
}

/// The pointer as three Effs: window-pixel x/y, and 1 while the left button is held (0 otherwise). A step system
/// binds them — `gfx.mouse_x(handle)(mx:); gfx.mouse_y(handle)(my:); gfx.mouse_down(handle)(down:)` — so e.g. a
/// button device can hit-test its rect. Backends with no pointer (headless) report 0.
mouse_x :: func(h: window) -> Eff(int) {
  return gfx_be_mouse_x(h);
}
mouse_y :: func(h: window) -> Eff(int) {
  return gfx_be_mouse_y(h);
}
mouse_down :: func(h: window) -> Eff(int) {
  return gfx_be_mouse_down(h);
}

/// The horizontal scroll delta accumulated since the last read (drain-and-clear like `key`), fed by the mouse
/// wheel and a touch swipe. A step system folds it into a scroll position — `gfx.scroll(handle)(d:)`. Positive
/// = scroll the world right. Backends with no wheel/touch (headless) report 0.
scroll :: func(h: window) -> Eff(int) {
  return gfx_be_scroll(h);
}

/// WHO OWNS THE KEYBOARD — 1 if a native TEXT FIELD outside gfx currently has focus, 0 if gfx does.
///
/// A driver that embeds a real text widget (the web backend's editor is an actual DOM <textarea>) has two
/// consumers for one keyboard, and gfx CANNOT arbitrate it alone: on the web the browser routes keys straight
/// into the focused element, so gfx never sees them, and the driver never sees them either. `axis_x` and `key`
/// therefore go SILENT whenever a text field owns the keyboard — which is correct, but leaves the driver
/// unable to tell "no key is pressed" from "someone else is getting the keys". This reports which it is, so a
/// driver can hold a real focus flag and gate its own input (don't walk the player while the user is typing).
///
/// Backends with no foreign text widget (x11 / wayland / headless) always report 0: gfx owns the keyboard,
/// and focus is entirely the driver's business.
text_focus :: func(h: window) -> Eff(int) {
  return gfx_be_text_focus(h);
}

/// RELEASE the keyboard back to gfx: blur whatever text field holds it and focus the gfx surface. Returns 1 if
/// it actually took focus back, 0 if gfx already had it. The driver calls this when the user clicks away from
/// an embedded text widget — the counterpart to `text_focus`. A no-op (0) on backends with no text widget.
release_text :: func(h: window) -> Eff(int) {
  return gfx_be_release_text(h);
}

/// Is the primary pointer COARSE — i.e. is this a touch device? Standard CSS `pointer: coarse`, which asks
/// about the input's PRECISION, not the screen size: a narrow desktop window stays fine-pointered, and a phone
/// held in landscape stays coarse. A driver uses this to decide whether to show on-screen controls, since a
/// touch device has no keyboard. Backends with a real mouse (x11 / wayland / headless) always report 0.
coarse_pointer :: func(h: window) -> Eff(int) {
  return gfx_be_coarse_pointer(h);
}

/// Fill the framebuffer buffer with the window's `bg` colour (0xRRGGBB). The framebuffer + window are `[1]`
/// singletons; the outer system binds the shared buffer, the nested fan iterates the (one) window for `bg`/dims.
clear :: system (query { framebuffer })(framebuffer) eff {
  map (query { handle, bg }) eff {
    dims(handle)(w:, h:);
    n := w * h;
    for (i := 0; i < n; i += 1) {
      framebuffer[i] = bg;
    }
  };
}

/// Present the framebuffer buffer to the window (`px` is in-out per the FFI borrow rule; present only reads it).
present :: system (query { framebuffer }) eff {
  map (query { handle }) eff {
    dims(handle)(w:, h:);
    gfx_be_present(handle, framebuffer, w, h)(_:);
  };
}

/// A window-close event as data: empty while open, one row once closed. The driver owns `[1]Closed` and
/// reacts (e.g. an `map (Closed) eff` that halts). `at` is the close stamp.
Closed :: arche { at }

poll :: system eff {
  map (query { handle }) eff {
    gfx_be_poll(handle)(r:);
    if (!r) {
      insert(Closed { at: 0 })(_:, _ok:);
    }
  };
}
